Restart options
------------------------------------------------------------

:restart-after-termination?
---------------------------

When this option is true, start! may start a new worker
generation for a component in :terminated. Without the
option, a component whose worker terminated normally cannot
be started again.

  {:restart-after-termination? true}


:restart-after-failure?
-----------------------

When this option is true, start! may start a new worker
generation for a component in :failed. The previously stored
failure is cleared for the new start. Without the option,
start! exposes the previous failure as its cause.

  {:restart-after-failure? true}

Both options are disabled by default and accept boolean
values. They affect only start! in their respective terminal
states. A worker that was explicitly stopped can always be
started again from :stopped.


Lifecycle notifications and generations
------------------------------------------------------------

Use :on-transit to register a function that receives a map
for every state transition:

  {:on-transit
   (fn [{:keys [generation from to cause]}]
     (println "Generation" generation ":" from "->" to)
     (when cause
       (println "Cause:" cause)))}

Each notification contains the following keys:

  * :generation is the worker generation number. It starts
    at 1 for the first start and increases whenever a new
    worker is actually created.
  * :from is the previously reported state.
  * :to is the new state.
  * :cause is the original throwable and is present only
    when :to is :failed.

Callbacks run asynchronously, but in the same order as the
state transitions. A slow callback therefore does not block
start!, stop!, or the worker. Callback errors are caught so
that subsequent notifications can still be delivered.

Listeners can also be added and removed while the component
is running:

  (def listener #(println "transition:" %))

  (supervise/add-listener! service listener)
  (supervise/remove-listener! service listener)

Listeners run asynchronously. Registering the same listener
more than once has no effect, and their invocation order is
unspecified. Removing an unknown listener has no effect.
Because delivery is asynchronous, an event already queued
when a listener is removed may still be delivered to it.


